<a2ui>
root=Column([a,d,i,t,aa,an,as,at],gap="md",padding=16)
a=Column([b,c],"sm")
b=Text("Mechanism","h2")
c=Text("Gene therapy treats disease by delivering a therapeutic gene, or by replacing, supplementing, or silencing a faulty gene; it does not necessarily cut the genome at a specific DNA sequence.[2][14]","caption")
d=Column([e,f],"sm")
e=Text("CRISPR gene editing uses a guide RNA to bring a Cas nuclease to a chosen DNA site, where it creates a double-strand break; repair by NHEJ often disrupts a gene, while HDR can make a precise sequence change.","caption")
f=Text("In practice, CRISPR is a gene-editing platform that can be used inside a gene-therapy strategy, but the terms are not interchangeable.","caption")
g=Column([h,i],"sm")
h=Text("Delivery methods","h2")
i=Column([j,k,l],"sm")
j=Text("Gene therapy commonly uses viral vectors, especially adeno-associated virus and lentiviral systems, because they efficiently carry therapeutic DNA into cells.","caption")
k=Text("This delivery approach can cause immunogenic toxicity and, with integrating vectors, insertional oncogenesis.","caption")
l=Text("CRISPR can be delivered by viral vectors, lipid nanoparticles, or by direct delivery of Cas protein and guide RNA as a ribonucleoprotein complex; the core challenge is getting the editing machinery into the right cells long enough to act.","caption")
m=Column([n,o],"sm")
n=Text("Established clinical examples","h2")
o=Column([p,q,r],"sm")
p=Text("Approved gene therapy includes several established products for inherited disease and hematologic disorders, such as AAV-based gene replacement and lentiviral ex vivo modification; these are now standard clinical categories rather than experimental concepts.","caption")
q=Text("Approved CRISPR-based therapy is now also a reality: ex vivo CRISPR editing of patient cells has reached clinical approval in some regions for severe hemoglobin disorders, showing that CRISPR is no longer limited to research.","caption")
r=Text("By contrast, germline CRISPR editing of embryos or reproductive cells is not an approved clinical treatment and remains broadly restricted.[3][10]","caption")
s=Column([t,u],"sm")
t=Text("Limitations","h2")
u=Column([v,w,x],"sm")
v=Text("Gene therapy may give durable benefit, but it can be limited by immune responses, vector size constraints, and imperfect control of where and how strongly the added gene is expressed.","caption")
w=Text("CRISPR offers site-specific editing, but limitations include off-target edits, incomplete editing, dependence on repair pathways, and difficulty achieving uniform correction in all target cells.","caption")
x=Text("Both approaches face major delivery barriers, but CRISPR adds an extra layer of risk because a cut can create unintended DNA changes rather than simply adding a gene.","caption")
y=Column([z,aa],"sm")
z=Text("Ethical questions","h2")
aa=Column([ab,ac,ad],"sm")
ab=Text("For somatic treatments, ethics focuses mainly on safety, efficacy, informed consent, and fair access.[6][7]","caption")
ac=Text("For germline editing, the concerns become sharper: future people cannot consent, edits may be inherited, and there are worries about unpredictable harms, enhancement uses, and eugenics.[8][12]","caption")
ad=Text("Broader concerns also include justice, affordability, regulation, and whether powerful editing tools will widen health inequities.[1]","caption")
ae=Column([af,ag],"sm")
af=Text("Approved vs. experimental","h2")
ag=Column([ah,ai],"sm")
ah=Text("Approved today: conventional gene therapies and some somatic CRISPR therapies.","caption")
ai=Text("Experimental or highly restricted: embryo editing, germline editing, enhancement applications, and many in vivo CRISPR programs still in trials.","caption")
an=Text("Current data on long-term safety and population-level effects remain limited, so many advanced CRISPR applications are still under close study rather than routine care.","caption")
